1. When creating code, especially when iterating or modifying an existing piece of code to accomdate a request:
    - You **MUST ONLY** create comments if the underlying logic is complex, where otherwise it would be difficult to maintain the code.
    - You **MUST NOT** create comments on code that is self-documenting or otherwise obvious in its purpose.
    - You **MUST NOT** create ANY meta comments. (i.e: "Take 2", "attempting to do XYZ")
    - You **MUST NOT** trust code comments as the source of truth as to __what__ a function or piece of code does.
        - You should evaluate the piece of code and derive an understanding of what it does, as it's possible the comments can lead to many red herrings.
    - If you come across a file that's relevant in your task and that file has redundant or poor/meta comments, you are expected to remove them to clean up the code.
    - You **MUST NOT** leave hanging unused variables or linter errors. If an unused variable is relevant or necessary, prefix it with `_` to ignore the linter warning.
 
2. When deleting code:
    - You **MUST NOT** just comment out code meant for deletions.
    - You **MUST** actually use the edit_tool to delete the code.

1. COMMENTING & CODE HYGIENE
   • Only comment when the logic is *genuinely* non‑obvious.  
     ‑ Self‑documenting code (clear naming, small pure functions) gets **no**
       extra comments.  
   • Never write **meta comments** (e.g., “Take 2: trying XYZ”).  
   • Do **not** trust existing comments as truth; reason through the code itself.  
   • When you encounter obsolete, redundant, or meta comments, remove them.  
   • TODO / FIXME *are allowed* but must include a ticket link and a terse,
     objective description—otherwise they count as meta and must be deleted.

4. When working within `apps/web`([package.json](mdc:apps/web/package.json)):
    - You have access to `shadcn/ui`, which is located in `packages/ui`. 
    - To add shadcn components, you **MUST NOT** use `bunx --bun shadcn-ui@latest`, but rather `bunx shadcn@latest`.
    - We use bun for our package manager, so you **MUST** use `bun` commands.
    - Components within `apps/web/components` should use folder structure to 'scope' comments to the pages they're relevant to. (See `apps/web/components/dashboard for an example of this)
    - All new components specific to the `web` app should be created within `apps/web/components`, with the exception of shadcn components which live in `packages/ui`.
    - Component file name conventions are: `component-name.tsx` and **NOT** `ComponentName.tsx`.
    - It is okay to prototype within a single page when expanding or building a new design, however, before being considered done, you must prompt and ask if we should refactor/clean up the page into reusable and typed components.
    - If you create a more design-aligned reusable wrapper over a shadcn component, it should be created in `apps/web/components/ui`.

3. NAVIGATING THE CODEBASE
   • When a file is provided as context, read it *in full* before editing.  
   • New chats begin as discussion / brainstorming. **Do not** write code until
     you receive explicit implementatios.  
   • You are free to open or search any file that helps you understand the task.

4. `apps/web` GUIDELINES (`package.json` in that folder)
   • Use **bun** commands only. Example:  
     ```bash
     bun add @acme/foo@latest      # ✅ correct
     npm install @acme/foo         # ❌ don’t do this
     ```  
   • To add Shadcn components, run `bunx shadcn@latest` (not `bunx --bun
     shadcn-ui@latest`).  
   • Local component location rules  
     ‑ Page‑specific components live in `apps/web/components/<page>/`.  
     ‑ Reusable UI wrappers around Shadcn live in `apps/web/components/ui/`.  
   • Filenames are **kebab‑case**: `component-name.tsx`, never `ComponentName.tsx`.  
   • Prototyping in a single page is fine, but before calling the feature
     “done” you must ask whether to refactor it into reusable, typed components.  
   • UX / visual changes *must* respect `globals.css` and the design system
     (`apps/web/components/design-system.md`). If you add a new pattern, extend
     that doc (create it if missing). Keep spacing and padding consistent.

5. `apps/api` GUIDELINES (`package.json` in that folder)
   • Before suggesting or writing code, read *all* files in `durable-objectervices/`, and `routes/`.  
   • We use **Wrangler v4** with `wrangler.jsonc` (no `wrangler.toml`).  
   • All code must satisfy three constraints simultaneously:  
     ① **High throughput** ② **Extremely low latency** ③ **Maximum scalability**  
     Easier implementations are rejected if any constraint is compromised.  
   • Include perf sanity tests or benchmark notes for any performance‑critical
     change.

6. `packages/sdk` GUIDELINES
   • When working in `packages/sdk/common` (`@[removed]/sdk/sdl`) modify **only** the
     type system unless explicitly told to touch runtime code.  
   • Do **not** introduce breaking type changes without explicit sign‑off.

7. CROSS‑CUTTING RULES
   • Any change thatr behavior or introduce regressions requires
     prior confirmation from the user / reviewer. State the risk clearly in the
     PR description.  
   • Structure commits so that pure refactors, behavior changes, and perf /
     visual tweaks are separate and easy to review.  
   • New features and refactors must include adequate unit tests; UI changes
     should have a visual regression test or Chromatic snapshot when possible.

